{"kind":"artifact","text":"Read the repository Git was pointed at, not the one the environment names. Exact clean pushed head f6335179d5b8d0e908bf4cf0bf7211d7bd79d1be on request/repo-remote-link; tree f08ef41f, checkout clean, origin matching, base equals current main c679fb73. Under planner's current-basis request eb034253 and my promise 8abe2015.\n\nTHE DEFECT I SHIPPED AND CODEX CAUGHT. I made `git config --local` the fix for reading only this repository's remotes, and published a contract saying the read is repository-local. --local bounds which configuration file is consulted. It does not bound which repository Git resolves. With GIT_COMMON_DIR pointing elsewhere, the exact command returned another repository's remote, exit 0. Every downstream defence — the allowlists, the bounds, the browser's refusal — sat downstream of a value that could already have come from somewhere else.\n\nWIDER THAN THE VERDICT SAID. Codex named the remote read. Five call sites in this package inherit the environment, and all five are redirectable: the config read, `worktree list`, `rev-parse --show-toplevel`, the per-worktree `git status` that decides clean or dirty, and the `git grep` behind citingDocuments. GIT_WORK_TREE makes the served path itself report the attacker's directory. GIT_INDEX_FILE makes a clean checkout report \"unavailable\".\n\nONE OF THEM IS A GUARD BYPASS, NOT A DISPLAY DEFECT. GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS and GIT_NOGLOB_PATHSPECS turn citingDocuments' `*.md` pathspec into a literal filename matching nothing. The citation gate then returns an empty answer, and an empty answer is what lets a cited retirement land. So the pathspec family is stripped alongside the routing family, and the comment says why.\n\nTHE FIX. gitRoutingVariables and repositoryLocalGit, one constructor, applied at all five sites. The only remaining bare git invocation in this package is inside the constructor itself.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT STRIPPED, because it looks like an omission and is not. GIT_CONFIG and GIT_CONFIG_* stay. internal/gitstore and internal/testgit pin the system and global config to /dev/null through exactly that family, and safe.directory — which decides whether git will read a repository owned by another user at all — normally lives in the scope they point at. Stripping them would remove protection rather than add it. I measured that core.worktree injected through GIT_CONFIG_COUNT does not redirect `rev-parse --show-toplevel` or `worktree list`, so the config family is not a routing vector here.\n\nTHE SECOND BLOCKER. webRemoteURL now tests the raw string for \"?\" and \"#\" before net/url.Parse sees it, and the post-parse check is gone rather than kept beside it. Go's parser drops a bare trailing \"#\" and re-serializes without it, so the old check admitted \"https://host/repo.git#\" while the browser preserved the delimiter and refused. Checking raw bytes is both the fix and the simplification.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, run and watched, tree restored clean after each. Reverting the config call site alone fails TestLocalWorktreesIgnoresInheritedGitRoutingVariables/{GIT_DIR,GIT_COMMON_DIR} and the matching service subtests. Reverting the grep site fails TestCitingDocumentsIgnoresInheritedGitRoutingVariables/{GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS,GIT_NOGLOB_PATHSPECS,GIT_DIR} with \"hid the citing page: got [], want [page.md]\". Reverting webRemoteURL to the post-parse check fails TestWebRemoteURLAdmitsOnlyHTTPAndHTTPS by name.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT TAKE. internal/gitstore is redirectable the same way — cat-file returns another repository's object through a pinned --git-dir — and app.Open itself resolves through it. That is a kernel-boundary change this row does not authorize; it is planner's separate request d2605fd7, gated on a threat-model ruling from hugh. The regressions here open the workspace before setting the variable so they isolate these five sites, and the comments say so.\n\nPROVENANCE. All four lane commits were rewritten to rest on eb034253, planner's current-basis replacement for the staled 3b06d237, with the tree preserved byte for byte at f08ef41f. Nothing about the work changed; only what authorizes it. This supersedes cff9d9e1, the internal/app artifact I published at 905e0b05 before stopping — that one was stale at birth because my earlier promise had been world-staled by merge 6bb01a86, and seven such artifacts plus a verdict resting on them would have handed the merging actor a set they could not use.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 229.171s, internal/kernel 157.364s, internal/app 157.137s, internal/docset 99.737s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 92.132s. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0. The tree is byte-identical to the pre-re-anchor head 905e0b05, so these numbers could not have differed — I ran them anyway rather than write \"green at\" a commit I had not gated.","body":{"commit":"f6335179d5b8d0e908bf4cf0bf7211d7bd79d1be","path":"internal/app"}}